# Presentation deck. The sources are tracked; the generated and copied
# artefacts below are not. The split is deliberate rather than tidy-minded:
# `deploy-sandbox.ps1` runs `git add --all`, so anything here that is untracked
# but not ignored is pushed to main and re-fetched onto the 1 GiB build box.
deck/node_modules/
deck/dist/
deck/.deck-qa/
deck/.deck-shots/
deck/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# `deck/public/art/` is a byte-identical copy of `frontend/public/art/` (218
# .webp, 18 MB), which is already tracked — see `deck/src/app-art/PORT.md`. One
# `cp -R` rebuilds it exactly, and deck/README.md records the command.
#
# `deck/public/stills/` is NOT ignored, despite being 26 MB of PNG. Nothing in
# the repo regenerates it: the route stills were captured by hand against a
# seeded account that no longer exists in that state, and the two office-tier
# stills came from `.shots-keep/`, which is ignored two blocks up. They are the
# deck's on-stage fallback, so losing them costs more than carrying them.
deck/public/art/

# Root-level harness dependencies (playwright, pngjs, gifenc) used by the
# scratch probes above. The application's own manifests live in frontend/.
/package.json
/package-lock.json

# Local dev worktrees symlink this to a shared install; the trailing-slash rule above does not match a symlink.
frontend/node_modules

# Every scratch directory named above is a dot directory at the repository root,
# and so is every one found in the eight sibling worktrees on this machine. The
# list has now missed three: ~810 MB and ~420 MB were caught by hand just before
# a release, and `.qa-run/` (27 MB) was committed and had to be untracked. A name
# has to be added at the moment the directory is created, by whoever is deep in
# something else, or `deploy-sandbox.ps1` sweeps it into main with `git add --all`.
#
# So the default at the root is inverted: a directory whose name begins with a
# dot is scratch unless something says otherwise. This is safe because an ignore
# rule cannot untrack anything. `.qa-tmp/` and `.verify/` are the only root dot
# directories with tracked contents — the parity fixtures and the benchmark
# harnesses — and both are already ignored by name above, so their tracked files
# stay tracked and nothing about them changes. Dot *files* are not matched, since
# the trailing slash restricts the rule to directories, so `.qa-report.md`,
# `.firm-audit.md` and the rest of the write-ups stay visible as before.
#
# It is anchored to the root on purpose, so scratch below the root stays listed
# by name — `deck/.deck-qa/` and `deck/.deck-shots/` above, `frontend/.rig-*`
# further up. A nested dot directory is not reliably scratch: a published
# `frontend/public/.well-known/` is the kind of thing the mobile client asks for,
# and it would be a bad thing to lose silently.
/.*/

# Shared configuration that has to be versioned if it is ever added. Nothing
# lives here today; the exception is written down now so that the rule above
# cannot one day swallow CI without anyone noticing.
!/.github/

# A database is scratch wherever it is written, and the rule above cannot see
# it. That rule inverts the default for dot directories *at the root*, so it is
# blind by construction to a database a harness leaves beside the application:
# `backend/mapgen-qa.db`, with its `-wal` and `-shm` sidecars, was 51 MiB of a
# copied dev database — 195 user rows and 3,139 auth sessions — and it reached
# an integration branch bound for a remote that is public. `.qa-run/firm.db`
# arrived the same way and by a different route. Both were caught by hand.
#
# Untracking one of these after the fact does not undo it: the blob stays in
# the tree of the commit that added it and travels with any push of that
# branch, so the only real remedy is to keep it out in the first place. That is
# what this block is for, and it matters more than the size does, because
# `deploy-sandbox.ps1` runs `git add --all` before it commits the release.
#
# Nothing versioned is at risk. The application's own database lives in
# `backend/instance/`, ignored by name at the top of this file, and no tracked
# file anywhere in the tree matches these shapes. An ignore rule cannot untrack
# anything, so a database that is deliberately versioned later stays versioned;
# it just has to be added with `git add -f`, which is the point.
*.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
